body:
## 2026-09-07 route-change and pressure acceptance
Compose existing P3AI navigation, P3TowerDefense combat/wave authority, Earthworks #1012 and placement #1033/#2440. Reuse current #2603 wave-phase WIP.

Test one real lane sequence: baseline creep route -> valid terrain/defense change -> nav/collision update -> alternate route/declared failure -> destruction or undo -> route reopened. Define the pending-update behavior; creeps must not tunnel, attack an unreachable stale Core location, freeze indefinitely or instantly teleport through the obstruction. A final-path rejection/rollback must preserve valid world/economy state.

Core relocation invalidates relevant goals; spawn/defeat and wave pressure remain server-owned. Declare target selection and no-path recovery in current feature data/logic. Do not add a second navigation registry. Local nav generation/invokers are an option only if #2611 identifies the existing generation strategy as a cost problem.

Show both teams' pressure and the player's ability to change it in normal play. Measure edit-to-valid-route latency on the fixed workload; no invented universal timing threshold. Enemy behavior and navigation updates serialize at their actual .cpp owners; scenario/evidence preparation can proceed independently.

---

Parent: #803 / #941.

Make the live Lane War feel like a lane battle rather than a systems sandbox. Reuse the existing creep, faction, navigation, damage and Core authorities; do not create another wave/combat system.

Acceptance:
- both teams continuously create visible lane pressure;
- opposing creeps meet, fight, die and continue toward the enemy Core;
- surviving creeps can damage the Core and player actions can materially change that pressure;
- blockers/terrain changes use the existing navigation behavior instead of bypassing it;
- Core destruction produces the existing replicated match result;
- verify through the normal playable Lane War path, not a new proof harness.
